<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Terra-Realm-Measurer: Lares (Tasked Spirit). A blind, read-only MEASUREMENT for a design pass. Read only the pinned commit: `git -C /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/s2b/wt show 3293533a4:<path>` (another writer works in that tree; never read its working files).
Ruling (operator 2026-10-29, "PAGE NEVER A VESSEL"): every page holds a SURFACE with a PORT onto a vessel; no sovereign VM and no key on a surface; a browser vessel lives in its workers (islands, the opened key). Today `packages/lararium-web/index.html` loads `src/main.ts` as a page module and `main.ts` (~527) calls `openBrowserVessel` (`packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts` ~478, 2082 lines) on the PAGE MAIN THREAD. Workers exist: `packages/lararium-web/src/workers/{daemon,shared-holder,wiki}.worker.ts`, `packages/lararium-browser/src/{shared-holder.worker,ward.worker,browser-wiki-worker,worker-handle}.ts`.
MEASURE, exhaustively, with file:line:
# Every value that lives in the PAGE realm today and the ruling forbids there: keys, seeds, passphrases/routes, custody tree keys, unlocked keystore handles, the IDB keystore itself (opened from the page?), the vessel's sovereign composition (signing, folding, admission, founding, fleet join, handle acts, the ward), the Automerge repo, sockets/dials to a hearth.
# For each: which function runs it, called from where, and which existing worker could already host it (what that worker already holds and does).
# What the page legitimately needs: the DOM, the TW5 mirror (DreamDOM D3), the custody/ward ask dialogs (painting a form), navigation. What must cross the port both ways (asks, answers, frames, events).
# Idempotence: what a page reload, a second tab, a crashed worker or a retried ask re-runs today, and whether it lands anything twice.
# What the node vessel composes (`packages/lararium-node`: the floor-then-lift capability record, `prepareNodeBootOn` or its successor) that the browser composes differently, by name.
Output (final message, under 900 words): the measured table (value · runs in · called from · could live in), the port's traffic, the idempotence findings, the node/browser composition diff. MEASURED vs INFER per row. No design proposals.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

